        <div id="measure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Measure a Narrow Window for an Air Conditioner
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every narrow window air conditioner purchase starts with a tape measure, and most bad purchases start without one. Do not trust the nominal window size from the builder or the listing for your replacement windows. Measure the actual clear opening the unit will sit in, at the narrowest point, with the sash fully open. Window frames are rarely perfectly square, so measure the width at the bottom, middle and top and use the smallest number.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a double-hung window, which opens by sliding the lower sash up, you need the width between the side jambs or tracks, and the height from the sill to the bottom of the raised sash. Most standard window units need at least 21 to 23 inches of width and 12 to 14 inches of height. The Midea U and most inverter units need about 22 inches. If your window is narrower than 21 inches, a standard window unit will not fit, full stop.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a horizontal slider, which slides sideways, measure the width of the open half and the full height of the opening. These openings tend to be tall and narrow, and the vertical casement/slider units from Frigidaire need about 15.5 inches of width and roughly 21.5 to 22.5 inches of height. For a casement, which cranks outward on hinges, measure the opening with the sash fully open, and check whether the crank hardware, screen or a center mullion will block the unit.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Window Opening Minimums from Spec Sheets</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15.5 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Frigidaire slider/casement units, min width</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">21 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">LG LW5016, min width</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">22 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Midea U-Shaped, min width</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">23 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Frigidaire 5,000 BTU, min width</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Also measure the sill depth and the wall thickness, and check what is outside. Storm windows, aluminum siding, a narrow exterior sill or a balcony railing can all block a unit that fits the opening on paper. The casement units list a maximum wall thickness of about 8.5 inches, which matters for thick masonry walls. Write all of these numbers down before you shop, and compare them with the installation manual for the exact model, not just the listing headline.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="window-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Double-Hung, Slider or Casement: Which Unit Fits Which Window
          </h2>

          <p>
            The window type decides the category before BTU or features do. A standard window air conditioner is a horizontal box designed for a double-hung window, where the lower sash closes down onto the top of the unit. If you have that kind of window and it is at least 21 inches wide, you have the most choice: basic 5,000 BTU units, compact inverter units and the U-shaped design all work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Horizontal sliders, common in apartments, basements and newer homes, are the classic problem. The opening is tall and narrow when the sash slides aside, and a horizontal box would leave a large gap above it. Some people stand a standard unit in a slider with a tall filler panel, but it is a poor seal and often unstable. The right answer is a vertical slider/casement unit like the Frigidaire FFRS0822SE, which is built for that tall, narrow shape and comes with a panel to fill the space above.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casement windows, which crank outward, are the hardest. Nothing sits in a casement the way a unit sits in a double-hung, because there is no sash to close down on it. Casement-specific units are installed in the open frame with a rigid filler panel, and some installs require removing the sash for the season. This is where many people decide a portable air conditioner with a casement window kit or a ductless mini-split is simpler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brand notes matter here. Midea's U-shaped models only fit double-hung windows. LG and Frigidaire sell conventional small units for double-hung windows, and Frigidaire is the main mainstream brand still selling slider/casement units. Koldfront sold well-regarded casement models, but several have been discontinued. hOmeLabs and Soleus Air sell conventional and saddle-style window units with good reputations, but we did not find a current true casement model from either, so check any listing that claims casement compatibility carefully.
          </p>

          <p>
            If no window unit fits, look at the alternatives honestly. A portable air conditioner exhausts through a hose and a window kit and fits almost any window, but it is louder, less efficient and takes floor space. A through-the-wall unit needs a hole cut in the wall. A ductless system from our <a href="/best-mini-split-heat-pump/">best mini-split heat pumps</a> guide costs more up front but is quieter and far more efficient than any window unit, and it frees the window entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="btu-sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. BTU Sizing: How Much Air Conditioner a Small Room Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Narrow windows push people toward the smallest units, so it is worth knowing exactly what each size can handle. The table below follows the widely used ENERGY STAR room air conditioner sizing guidance. Start with the room's square footage, then adjust for sun, occupants and kitchens.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room size (sq. ft.)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Recommended capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical narrow-window option</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100 to 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5,000 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">LG LW5016, Frigidaire FHWC054TE1</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">150 to 250</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6,000 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">LG LW6023IVSM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">250 to 300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7,000 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Compact 7,000 BTU units</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">300 to 350</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8,000 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Midea U 8,000, Frigidaire FFRS0822SE</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">350 to 400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9,000 BTU</td>
