                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Frigidaire FFRS1022RE, Midea U 10,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">450 to 550</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12,000 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Midea U 12,000</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Adjust from there. Add about 10 percent if the room gets strong sun, and subtract about 10 percent if it is heavily shaded. Add roughly 600 BTU for each regular occupant beyond two. If the unit is cooling a kitchen, add about 4,000 BTU, because cooking adds a lot of heat. Top-floor rooms under a hot roof and rooms with large west-facing windows often need a step up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not oversize. A unit that is too big for the room cools the air quickly and shuts off before it has removed much humidity, leaving the room cold and clammy. It also cycles on and off more often, which is noisier and wears the compressor. That is one reason inverter units are popular: they can run slowly for long periods, which dehumidifies better even if the capacity is a little generous.
          </p>

          <p>
            Undersizing has the opposite problem. A 5,000 BTU unit in a 250-square-foot room will run constantly on a hot afternoon and still fall behind. If your window only fits a small unit but the room is larger, you may be better served by a slider/casement unit with more capacity, a mini-split, or a combination of the window unit and a ceiling fan to spread the cool air. If you live in a dry climate, an evaporative cooler from our <a href="/best-evaporative-cooler/">best evaporative coolers</a> guide can supplement a small AC for far less power.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Inverter vs Standard Window Units
          </h2>

          <p>
            A standard window air conditioner has a fixed-speed compressor. It runs at full power until the thermostat is satisfied, shuts off, and starts again a few minutes later when the room warms. Each start is a clunk and a surge of noise, and the temperature swings a degree or two around the set point. It is a simple, proven design, and it is why basic units are inexpensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            An inverter unit uses a variable-speed compressor. It runs hard to pull the room down, then slows down and keeps running at low speed to hold the temperature. The result is a steadier room, better humidity removal, and much less noise, because the compressor rarely switches off and on. Inverter units also tend to use less electricity over a season, especially in rooms where the cooling load varies through the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is cost. Inverter window units like the Midea U and the LG Dual Inverter often cost two to three times as much as a basic 5,000 or 6,000 BTU unit. If you sleep in the room and run the AC most nights for several months a year, the quieter operation alone is often worth the premium, and the energy savings help. If it is a guest room or an office you cool a few weeks a year, a basic unit is the better buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inverter units also tend to be slightly larger and to have higher minimum window widths. That is why the narrowest windows usually end up with basic units: the LG LW5016 fits a 21-inch opening, while most inverter models want 22 inches or more. If your window is right on the line, measure twice, and remember that a unit you cannot fit is not a bargain at any efficiency.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an apples-to-apples efficiency comparison, look at the Combined Energy Efficiency Ratio, or CEER, on the yellow EnergyGuide label. Higher is better. ENERGY STAR certified units meet a higher efficiency bar than the federal minimum, and many inverter models carry it. A mini-split heat pump is more efficient still; our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-mini-splits-last/">how long mini-splits last</a> explains the long-term economics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Noise: What to Expect From a Small Window AC
          </h2>

          <p>
            Noise is the most common complaint about small window air conditioners, and it matters more in a narrow window because those units usually go into bedrooms. A window unit makes two kinds of noise: the indoor fan, which is a steady whoosh, and the compressor, which is a lower hum or rattle that starts and stops on a standard unit. The compressor is what wakes people up.
          </p>

          <p>
            The U-shaped design is the biggest step forward here. Because the indoor and outdoor halves are separated by the closed window, most of the compressor noise stays outside. Inverter units like the LG Dual Inverter reduce the problem differently, by running the compressor slowly and continuously instead of cycling it. Basic units are louder, and decibel ratings on boxes are often measured under favorable conditions, so treat them as rough guides.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation affects noise as much as the model. A unit that is not level, not firmly supported, or touching loose window trim will rattle and buzz. Foam weatherstripping between the sash and the unit, a solid support bracket and tight side panels all help. If a unit becomes noisier over time, check for loose screws, a fan blade striking debris, or a bent cabinet panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the fan settings and modes intelligently. Most units have a sleep mode that lowers fan speed and gradually raises the set point overnight. Running the fan on low and setting the thermostat a degree or two higher can make a basic unit far more tolerable. Some people run the unit hard before bed to cool the room, then switch to sleep mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            If noise is a deal-breaker, and you can afford it, the quietest option by far is a ductless mini-split, whose compressor sits outside entirely. For a quick comparison of other ways to move air quietly, our guide to <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">ceiling fans with lights</a> covers fans that can let you run a small AC at a higher set point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Installation and Support Brackets
          </h2>

          <p>
            A small window air conditioner is heavy for its size. Even a 5,000 BTU unit weighs roughly 35 to 40 pounds, and the 8,000 and 10,000 BTU casement units weigh considerably more. Most of that weight sits outside the window, which is why support matters. Every unit comes with brackets or a mounting kit, but for anything above the ground floor, an exterior support bracket that carries the weight to the wall is cheap insurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the manual. Each unit specifies a slight tilt toward the outside, usually about a quarter inch, so condensate drains outdoors rather than into the room. Set the unit on the sill, slide it into place, close the sash down onto the top rail or into the U channel, and secure the sash with the supplied lock or angle bracket so the window cannot be opened from outside. Extend the side panels, seal any gaps with the supplied foam, and plug the unit directly into a grounded outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            For upper floors, some cities have rules on how window units must be secured, and many landlords and co-op boards have their own. Even where there is no rule, a unit falling from a third-floor window is a real hazard. Use a bracket rated for the unit's weight, screw it into solid framing or masonry, and check the installation each spring before the first hot day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casement and slider installs need a little more planning. Slider units use an included panel above the unit, which may need trimming. Casement installs usually require a filler panel cut to fit around the unit and screwed or clamped to the frame; acrylic, plywood or insulated panels all work. Seal the edges with foam weatherstrip, and think about security on ground-floor windows. Our guides to <a href="/best-caulk-for-windows/">caulk for windows</a> and <a href="/how-to-caulk-exterior-windows/">caulking exterior windows</a> cover sealing frames properly afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical matters too. Plug window units directly into a wall outlet, never into a light-duty extension cord or power strip. Most small units run on a standard 115-volt outlet, but the circuit may also feed other loads, so avoid running a microwave or space heater on the same circuit. If the breaker trips regularly, the unit is sharing a circuit that is already busy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="efficiency" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Running Costs and Seasonal Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            A small window AC is one of the cheaper ways to cool a room. A 5,000 BTU unit typically draws around 400 to 500 watts while the compressor runs, so eight hours of steady running uses roughly 3 to 4 kWh, or about 50 to 70 cents a night at typical electricity rates. Inverter units often use less once the room is cool, because they slow down instead of cycling at full power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the filter every two weeks in season. A clogged filter cuts airflow, makes the unit work harder and can cause the coil to freeze. Most filters slide out from the front and rinse clean in the sink. At the start of each season, vacuum dust from the front grille and check that the drain path is clear. Mold growth in window units is common when they sit wet; run the fan for an hour after cooling to dry the coil.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of the season, either remove the unit and store it upright in a dry place, or cover the outside with a fitted insulated cover and seal the inside. Left in place uncovered, a window unit leaks cold air all winter and invites drafts and moisture. Casement and slider installs are more work to remove, so a good cover and an interior seal are often the practical choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the room is humid even when it is cool, the unit may be oversized, or the humidity source may be elsewhere. A dehumidifier from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/">best dehumidifiers for bedrooms</a> guide can help, and so can running the AC on a lower fan speed so air spends more time across the cold coil.
          </p>

          <p>
            During power outages, a window AC is a demanding load. Our guide to <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-an-air-conditioner/">whether a portable power station can run an air conditioner</a> covers what it takes; for most people a fan is the realistic outage plan and the AC is for when the grid returns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the Window Instead of the Opening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The nominal size of a window is not the clear opening a unit sits in. Measure the actual opening with the sash fully open at the bottom, middle and top, and use the smallest width. A quarter inch can be the difference between a clean install and a return trip to the store.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Standard Unit for a Slider</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standard window unit is a horizontal box made for double-hung windows. In a horizontal slider it leaves a tall gap above, seals poorly and is often unstable. Buy a vertical slider/casement unit for sliders and casements, or choose a portable AC or mini-split instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Oversizing for a Small Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bigger is not better. An oversized unit cools quickly and shuts off before removing much humidity, leaving the room cold and clammy, and it cycles more often, which is noisier. Size from the room's square footage, adjust for sun and occupants, and stop there.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Support Bracket</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most of a window unit's weight hangs outside the wall. Without a proper bracket, the sill and sash carry the load, and on upper floors a falling unit is a real danger. Use an exterior bracket rated for the unit's weight and anchor it into solid framing or masonry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It on an Extension Cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Window air conditioners draw a steady, heavy load. A light-duty extension cord or power strip can overheat. Plug the unit directly into a grounded wall outlet on a circuit that is not already busy with a microwave, space heater or other large appliance.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

