        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the smallest window air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Among standard window units, the LG LW5016 is one of the smallest, with retailers listing a 21-inch minimum window width and a cabinet about 17.3 inches wide. For even narrower openings, Frigidaire's slider/casement units need only 15.5 inches of width, but they are tall, so the opening needs about 21.5 to 22.5 inches of height.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a window AC in a casement window?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a casement-specific unit, or a standard unit with a fully built filler panel, which is less secure and seals poorly. Casement units like Frigidaire's slider/casement models sit in the open frame with a rigid panel around them. Many people find a portable air conditioner with a casement window kit or a ductless mini-split simpler.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will the Midea U fit a narrow window?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the window is a double-hung at least 22 inches wide and 13.75 inches tall, yes. The U shape lets the window close into the unit, which seals better and keeps most noise outside. It will not fit a casement or a horizontal sliding window, and some listings for larger models quote a 23-inch minimum width.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size air conditioner do I need for a small bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For 100 to 150 square feet, 5,000 BTU is usually enough. For 150 to 250 square feet, step up to 6,000 BTU. Add about 10 percent for a sunny room and roughly 600 BTU for each extra regular occupant beyond two. Avoid oversizing, which leaves the room cold but clammy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are inverter window air conditioners worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a bedroom you use most nights through the summer, usually yes. Inverter units run quietly and steadily instead of cycling, hold the temperature better and use less energy over a season. For a guest room or an office cooled only a few weeks a year, a basic unit costs far less and the savings never repay the difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a support bracket for a small window AC?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Above the ground floor, we strongly recommend one. Even a 5,000 BTU unit weighs around 35 to 40 pounds, mostly hanging outside the window. Some cities and many buildings require proper support for window units on upper floors. A bracket screwed into solid framing or masonry is inexpensive insurance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What if no window AC fits my window?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Consider a portable air conditioner with a window kit, which fits almost any window but is louder and less efficient; a through-the-wall unit, which needs a wall opening; or a ductless mini-split, which costs more up front but is the quietest and most efficient option and leaves the window free.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

